2026-09-14T05:06:32.5140490Z       "body": "### Summary\n\n`jsonable_encoder(some_bytes)` currently calls `some_bytes.decode()` with no error handling. For any `bytes` value that isn't valid UTF-8 (raw binary data — hashes, encrypted blobs, protobuf, DB blob columns), this raises an unhandled `UnicodeDecodeError` straight from deep inside FastAPI's internals, with no indication of what went wrong or how to fix it — even though a working escape hatch (`custom_encoder={bytes: ...}`) already exists and just isn't discoverable from the error.\n\n### Fix\n\nWrap the decode in a named `bytes_encoder()` function that re-raises as a `ValueError` (consistent with the `ValueError` `jsonable_encoder` already raises for other unencodable types) with a message that explains the problem and points at `custom_encoder` as the fix. The existing pattern of naming non-trivial encoders as top-level functions (e.g. `decimal_encoder`, `isoformat`) is followed rather than keeping an inline lambda.\n\n### Testing\n\nAdded three tests to `tests/test_jsonable_encoder.py`:\n- `test_encode_bytes_valid_utf8`: valid UTF-8 bytes still encode correctly (unchanged behavior)\n- `test_encode_bytes_invalid_utf8_raises_clear_error`: invalid UTF-8 now raises `ValueError` with an actionable message\n- `test_encode_bytes_invalid_utf8_with_custom_encoder`: the existing `custom_encoder` escape hatch still works and now has a regression test\n\nNot a breaking change for the success path; the only change is the exception type/message for previously-crashing input.",
